cjson is a lightweight C library for parsing and generating JSON. As an expert testing engineer, you need to evaluate the relevance of certain API functions in the cjson library to a target function. Given the target function's signature and a list of other function signatures from the library, consider the following questions for each function:

- Is the function designed to be used alongside the target function in certain scenarios?
- Do the function and the target function influence each other in a way that could modify their behavior?

If a function meets one or both of these criteria, it is considered relevant to the target function.
===
user:
---
The target function is `cJSON_Delete`. Frees a cJSON node and its owned descendants. Call it on a parsed/created item when done. It walks `next` siblings, recursively deletes `child`, and frees `valuestring`/`string` only if not marked `cJSON_IsReference`/`cJSON_StringIsConst`. Its signature is as follows:

```c
/* Delete a cJSON entity and all subentities. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void) cJSON_Delete(cJSON *item);
```

The list of candidate functions from the library is:

1. `cJSON_CreateString`
    Creates a new `cJSON` string item from a C string. Use it to build JSON values, then add/attach the returned item to an array/object. If allocation or string duplication fails, it deletes the item and returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateString(const char *string);
    ```
2. `cJSON_Parse`
    `cJSON_Parse` is a simple wrapper for `cJSON_ParseWithOpts(value, 0, 0)`. Use it to parse a JSON string into a `cJSON*` tree. On success, free with `cJSON_Delete`; on parse failure, it returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Memory Management: the caller is always responsible to free the results from all variants of cJSON_Parse (with cJSON_Delete) and cJSON_Print (with stdlib free, cJSON_Hooks.free_fn, or cJSON_free as appropriate). The exception is cJSON_PrintPreallocated, where the caller has full responsibility of the buffer. */
/* Supply a block of JSON, and this returns a cJSON object you can interrogate. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_Parse(const char *value);
    ```
3. `cJSON_PrintUnformatted`
    `cJSON_PrintUnformatted` serializes a `cJSON` item to compact JSON text with no whitespace/pretty-printing. Use it when you need transfer/storage output. It returns a newly allocated `char*` (or `NULL` on failure); free it with cJSON’s free hook. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Render a cJSON entity to text for transfer/storage without any formatting. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(char *) cJSON_PrintUnformatted(const cJSON *item);
    ```
4. `cJSON_SetNumberHelper`
    Helper for `cJSON_SetNumberValue`: set a cJSON number’s `valueint` and `valuedouble`. Use it on a number item. It clamps `valueint` to `INT_MAX/INT_MIN` when out of range, otherwise casts to `int`, and returns the stored `valuedouble`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* helper for the cJSON_SetNumberValue macro */
CJSON_PUBLIC(double) cJSON_SetNumberHelper(cJSON *object, double number);
    ```
5. `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts`
    Parses JSON from a buffer with an explicit length. Use it when input may not be NUL-terminated; `return_parse_end` gets the stop position. Fails on `NULL`/0 length or parse errors, and can require the input to end exactly at the JSON text. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts(const char *value, size_t buffer_length, const char **return_parse_end, cJSON_bool require_null_terminated);
    ```
6. `cJSON_AddArrayToObject`
    Creates a new empty JSON array and adds it to `object` under `name`. Use it for object members meant to hold arrays. On success returns the new array; on failure returns `NULL` and deletes the created array. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddArrayToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name);
    ```
7. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject`
    `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` removes an item from a JSON object by key and frees it. Use it on an object plus a member name. If the key exists, it’s detached then deleted; if not, nothing is deleted. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(void) cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject(cJSON *object, const char *string);
    ```
8. `cJSON_PrintPreallocated`
    `cJSON_PrintPreallocated` renders a `cJSON` value into a caller-provided buffer, avoiding internal allocation. Use it with a valid `buffer` and nonnegative `length`; `format` controls pretty-printing. It returns `1` on success, `0` if args are invalid or output doesn't fit. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Render a cJSON entity to text using a buffer already allocated in memory with given length. Returns 1 on success and 0 on failure. */
/* NOTE: cJSON is not always 100% accurate in estimating how much memory it will use, so to be safe allocate 5 bytes more than you actually need */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_PrintPreallocated(cJSON *item, char *buffer, const int length, const cJSON_bool format);
    ```
9. `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS`
    Adds `item` to `object` using a const key string. Use only for literals/long-lived strings; cJSON won’t copy/free the key and marks it const. Returns true/false via `add_item_to_object`; behavior depends on object/item validity and allocation success. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Use this when string is definitely const (i.e. a literal, or as good as), and will definitely survive the cJSON object.
 * WARNING: When this function was used, make sure to always check that (item->type & cJSON_StringIsConst) is zero before
 * writing to `item->string` */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
    ```
10. `cJSON_IsRaw`
    `cJSON_IsRaw` checks whether a `cJSON` item is a Raw node. Use it to test an item before treating it as raw JSON text. It returns `false` for `NULL`, otherwise `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_Raw`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsRaw(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
11. `cJSON_InsertItemInArray`
    Inserts `newitem` into `array` at index `which`. Use with a valid array and non-NULL item; negative index fails. If `which` is past the end, it appends. It also rejects corrupted list links. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Update array items. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_InsertItemInArray(cJSON *array, int which, cJSON *newitem);
    ```
12. `cJSON_Print`
    `cJSON_Print` renders a `cJSON` item into a newly allocated, formatted JSON text string using the global hooks. Use it to serialize a parsed/constructed tree for storage/transfer. It returns `NULL` on failure; free the result with the matching cJSON deallocator. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Render a cJSON entity to text for transfer/storage. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(char *) cJSON_Print(const cJSON *item);
    ```
13. `cJSON_CreateStringArray`
    Creates a cJSON array from `count` C strings. Use with a non-NULL `strings` pointer and `count >= 0`. Returns NULL if args are invalid, allocation fails, or any element creation fails; on failure it deletes the partially built array. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateStringArray(const char *const *strings, int count);
    ```
14. `cJSON_CreateTrue`
    `cJSON_CreateTrue()` creates a new cJSON boolean item set to JSON `true`. Use it when building a JSON tree and you need a true literal. It allocates a new node; returns `NULL` if allocation fails. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateTrue(void);
    ```
15. `cJSON_Version`
    Returns cJSON’s version as a static string like `major.minor.patch`. Call it with no args to query the library version; the returned pointer stays valid, but the buffer is reused on each call. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* returns the version of cJSON as a string */
CJSON_PUBLIC(const char*) cJSON_Version(void);
    ```
16. `cJSON_CreateIntArray`
    Creates a JSON array from `count` ints. Use with a valid `numbers` pointer and `count >= 0`; `count` must not exceed the source array length. Returns `NULL` on invalid args or allocation failure, and deletes the partially built array on failure. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* These utilities create an Array of count items.
 * The parameter count cannot be greater than the number of elements in the number array, otherwise array access will be out of bounds.*/
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateIntArray(const int *numbers, int count);
    ```
17. `cJSON_CreateArrayReference`
    Creates a cJSON array node that **references** an existing child list, not a copy. Use it when you want to wrap already-built items as an array without duplicating them. If allocation fails, returns `NULL`. The referenced child is cast away from `const`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateArrayReference(const cJSON *child);
    ```
18. `cJSON_ParseWithLength`
    `cJSON_ParseWithLength` is a convenience wrapper for parsing JSON from a buffer with a known length. Use it when the input may not be NUL-terminated. It simply calls `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts(value, buffer_length, 0, 0)`, so it parses the whole buffer and provides no return-parse-end info. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_ParseWithLength(const char *value, size_t buffer_length);
    ```
19. `cJSON_IsFalse`
    `cJSON_IsFalse` checks whether a `cJSON` item is the JSON boolean false. Use it on a `cJSON*` to test type safely. It returns `false` if `item == NULL`, otherwise `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_False`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsFalse(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
20. `cJSON_AddBoolToObject`
    Creates a JSON boolean child and adds it to an object under `name`. Use it with a valid object; it returns the new item on success. If insertion fails, it deletes the created bool and returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddBoolToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name, const cJSON_bool boolean);
    ```
21. `cJSON_IsObject`
    `cJSON_IsObject` checks whether a `cJSON*` is a non-NULL JSON object. Use it before treating `item` as an object. It returns `false` for `NULL` or any non-object type, and `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_Object`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsObject(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
22. `cJSON_DetachItemViaPointer`
    Detaches `item` from `parent`’s array/object without deleting it. Use only with a direct child of `parent`; returns `NULL` for NULL args or if `item` isn’t linked to `parent`. On success, links are fixed and the item is left standalone. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Remove/Detach items from Arrays/Objects. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_DetachItemViaPointer(cJSON *parent, cJSON * const item);
    ```
23. `cJSON_IsBool`
    `cJSON_IsBool` checks whether a `cJSON` item is a boolean (`true` or `false`). Use it to test an item before reading it as a bool. It returns `false` for `NULL` and for non-bool types; otherwise `true` if `type` has `cJSON_True` or `cJSON_False`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsBool(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
24. `cJSON_free`
    `cJSON_free` releases memory using cJSON’s configured deallocator (`global_hooks.deallocate`). Use it to free memory allocated by cJSON or via its hooks. It safely accepts `NULL` if the deallocator does. Setting `object = NULL` only affects the local copy. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(void) cJSON_free(void *object);
    ```


