2. `cJSON_AddStringToObject`
​ Adds a JSON string member to an object. Use with a valid `cJSON` object, key `name`, and C string `string`. It creates a string item, inserts it, and returns the new item. On failure, it deletes the item and returns `NULL`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*)
cJSON_AddStringToObject(cJSON* const object, const char* const name, const char* const string);
​ ```
3. `cJSON_AddNumberToObject`
​ Adds a numeric child to a cJSON object. Use it with a valid `object`, key `name`, and `double` value. It creates a number item, inserts it, and returns that item on success; on failure it deletes the item and returns `NULL`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*)
cJSON_AddNumberToObject(cJSON* const object, const char* const name, const double number);
​ ```
4. `cJSON_CreateObject`
​ `cJSON_CreateObject()` allocates a new empty `cJSON` item and sets its type to `cJSON_Object`. Use it to start building a JSON object, then add members with cJSON add-item APIs. If allocation fails, it returns `NULL`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *)
cJSON_CreateObject(void);
​ ```
5. `cJSON_CreateRaw`
​ Creates a `cJSON_Raw` item holding a copied raw JSON text string. Use it when you want cJSON to store/print preformatted JSON without parsing it. If allocation or string copy fails, it deletes the item and returns `NULL`.
